class:: FFTSpread2
summary:: Spectral standard deviation in Hz
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/SpecCentroid2, Classes/SpecFlatness2

description::
Returns sqrt(sum(w * (f - centroid).squared) / sum(w)), in Hz. The centroid uses the same weights and range.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. Peeters (2004), A Large Set of Audio Features for Sound Description; mathematical definitions are implemented independently. Source: link::https://recherche.ircam.fr/anasyn/peeters/ARTICLES/Peeters_2003_cuidadoaudiofeatures.pdf::.

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures spectral standard deviation in hz instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
Spectral spread measures the second central moment of the spectrum (the frequency standard deviation around the spectral centroid). Narrow harmonic sounds have low spread; wide noisy textures have high spread. In this example, spectral spread controls a stereo decorrelation delay network, widening broadband noisy components while focusing narrow sinusoidal partials in the center. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: displays real-time spectral spread in Hz.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("texture.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic texture fallback
            PinkNoise.ar * LFNoise1.kr(1).range(0.2, 0.8) + SinOsc.ar(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(200, 800), 0, 0.2)
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var spread = FFTSpread2.kr(chain, minFreq: 50, maxFreq: 15000, spectrumMode: 0, fftSize: 1024);
        var spreadNorm = (spread / 3500).clip(0, 1).lag(0.08);
        var delayL = DelayC.ar(source, 0.03, spreadNorm * 0.012);
        var delayR = DelayC.ar(source, 0.03, spreadNorm * 0.024);
        var sound = [
            source * (1 - (spreadNorm * 0.3)) + (delayL * spreadNorm * 0.4),
            source * (1 - (spreadNorm * 0.3)) + (delayR * spreadNorm * 0.4)
        ] * 0.25;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, spread.clip(0, 8000)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Spectral Spread", ~miraBus, 0.0, 5000.0, "Hz");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
